{% import "design/_example.html.jinja" as ex %}
{% macro demos() %}
    <p class="ds-lead">
        The per-book action popover: the four reading-log shelves, a star rating, and an "Add to list" pane that slides in with a filter and inline list creation. It composes <a href="#popover">Popover</a> for the shell; you supply the trigger. State is optimistic — the UI updates first, an error toast rolls it back — and every accepted change fires <code>ol-book-state-change</code> so the surrounding card can follow. Only render it for signed-in readers; send everyone else to log in.
    </p>
    {% call ex.example("Standalone", "Give it a book, the current shelf/rating, and the reader's key (needed to create lists). The trigger is whatever you slot in. Signed in, this demo opens on your real state for The Lord of the Rings and saves every change to your account.",
        code='<ol-book-actions user-key="/people/you">\n  <button slot="trigger">Save</button>\n</ol-book-actions>\n\n<script>\n  el.book = { key: "/works/OL27448W", title: "The Lord of the Rings",\n              firstPublishYear: 1954 };\n  el.shelf = 1; // Want to Read\n</script>') %}
        <ol-book-actions id="demo-book-actions" user-key="{{ user_key|default('', true) }}">
            <ol-button slot="trigger">
                Save
            </ol-button>
        </ol-book-actions>
        <p>Last change: <strong id="demo-book-actions-out">—</strong></p>
        <script>
            (function () {
                const el = document.getElementById('demo-book-actions');
                el.book = { key: '/works/OL27448W', title: 'The Lord of the Rings', firstPublishYear: 1954 };
                el.addEventListener('ol-book-state-change', (e) => {
                    document.getElementById('demo-book-actions-out').textContent = JSON.stringify(e.detail);
                });
                // Open on the reader's real shelf and rating — this demo writes
                // to their actual reading log, so it should show what's there.
                // The attribute, not the property: the element upgrades later.
                if (el.getAttribute('user-key')) {
                    fetch('/books-display/user-state.json?work_ids=OL27448W', { credentials: 'same-origin' })
                        .then((r) => (r.ok ? r.json() : null))
                        .then((state) => {
                            if (!state) return;
                            el.shelf = state.shelves.OL27448W ?? null;
                            el.rating = state.ratings.OL27448W ?? null;
                        })
                        .catch(() => {});
                }
            })();
        </script>
{% endcall %}
<div class="ds-notes">
    <p>
        <b>Requests.</b> Shelves post to <code>/works/OL…W/bookshelves.json</code>, ratings to <code>/works/OL…W/ratings.json</code>, lists through <code>/partials/MyBooksDropperLists.json</code> (read) and the lists/seeds endpoints (write). A 401 on any of them redirects to login.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Escape.</b> In the lists pane, Escape goes back to the main pane; a second Escape closes the popover.
    </p>
</div>
{% endmacro %}
